@namespace Fhi.Munin.Explorer.Blazor

@* Kelda, the kildeutforsker.

   No @page — the host owns routing; in helsedata's Optimizely CMS there is no router at all.
   No @rendermode — the host decides at the mount site, which is what lets this same component
   render under legacy Blazor Server and under a modern Blazor Web App.
   No CSS ships from this package.

   The names below split the way they do everywhere in this package. The page furniture wears
   Fhi.Helsedata.Stiler's own names — `headline`, `caption`, `form-element__label`,
   `searchbox__freetext*`, `hd-button-square` with its modifiers, `infobox` — and the structure
   wears the `munin-explorer` prefix this package owns. Four of those are the explorer's existing
   ones, reused rather than reinvented: the root `munin-explorer`, the `munin-explorer-container`
   and `munin-explorer-results` the list sits in, and `munin-explorer-drilldown` for the view an
   opened kilde takes over. Three are this view's own — `munin-explorer-kilder` for the table,
   `munin-explorer-kilder__name` for the control that opens a row and
   `munin-explorer-kilder__count` for the columns holding a number.

   The result list is a <table>, unlike the variable explorer's, and that is the shape rule rather
   than an inconsistency. Runa's rows are helsedata's own `munin-explorer-data-list`, a <ul> with a
   header row of <div>s, because that is the shape their stylesheet dresses and this component
   replaces their variable page. There is no equivalent to copy for a list of kilder, and eight
   columns of counts and names have to line up — so it is the element that already does that, for
   the reason the kodeverk code list and KildeView's datasamlinger are tables too: an element
   degrades to its own browser default where an unknown class name degrades to nothing. The name
   is a <button> for the same reason.

   No pager, no sortable headers and no URL state. All three are Kelda parity decisions recorded
   under the epic, not omissions — the endpoint is not paged, the API returns the list ordered by
   name, and the only shareable thing here is which kilde is open. See the component class.

   Accessibility: helsedata.no is a public-sector site, so WCAG 2.1 AA is a legal requirement, and
   this is our markup on their page. Visible focus and contrast are the host stylesheet's to get
   right; what markup can decide is decided here. *@

<section class="munin-explorer" aria-labelledby="@TitleId">
    @* Rendered from HeadingLevel rather than hard-coded to h2: the level that keeps the document
       outline unbroken is only knowable at the mount site. *@
    @Heading

@if (_selectedId is not null)
{
    @* A kilde opens as its own view, not as a panel inside a row. Runa navigates to a page for
       this; this component has no router, so instead it takes over its own area and offers a way
       back. The search text and the list are all still there on return because none of it was torn
       down — the list was never refetched to begin with.

       A region named after what it shows, so a screen reader moving by landmark hears which kilde
       it landed in. *@
    <div class="munin-explorer-drilldown"
         id="@DetailId"
         role="region"
         aria-labelledby="@DetailHeadingId"
         aria-busy="@DetailBusy">
        <button class="hd-button-square button-square--ghost margin-bottom"
                type="button"
                @onclick="CloseAsync">@T.BackToKilder</button>

        @* Once the kilde has arrived, KildeView owns the heading — the same component the variable
           explorer drills into, so the two cannot render one source two ways. Until then the view
           keeps its own: the region points at that id, and a landmark whose label does not exist
           yet is worse than a plain one. *@
        @if (_kilde is null)
        {
            @DrilldownHeading
        }

        <p class="@DetailStatusClass"
           role="status"
           aria-live="polite"
           aria-atomic="true">@DetailStatus</p>

        @if (_kilde is { } kilde)
        {
            @* Kelda's own sections go in through Sections, and Kelda's own word for the
               datasamling table goes in through DataCollectionsHeading — Runa says
               "Datasamlinger" over the same rows. Neither is markup added to KildeView, which is
               the whole reason that component is a core with slots. *@
            <KildeView Kilde="kilde"
                       Language="@Language"
                       HeadingLevel="@KildeLevel"
                       HeadingId="@DetailHeadingId"
                       DataCollectionsHeading="@T.HeadingDelkilderAndDataCollections"
                       Sections="@Sections" />
        }
    </div>
}
else
{
    @* The filter, read once for the whole render. Four things below want it — the count, the empty
       state, the table's own name and its rows — and the getter re-runs the Where and materialises
       a new list on every one of them. Nothing changes between them, so this is shape rather than
       correctness: it is how KildeView.DataCollectionTable opens (`var rows = DataCollections;`)
       and what the variable explorer's `@if (HierarchyCrumbs is { Count: > 0 } crumbs)` does. *@
    var visible = Visible;
    var summary = Summary(visible);
    var searchText = SearchText;

    @* role="search" makes this a search landmark, and aria-labelledby names it after this
       instance's title so two explorers on one page are told apart in a landmark list.

       There is nothing behind the submit — the filtering is done over a list already in hand — but
       the form is still a form: implicit submit on Enter is how a keyboard user finishes a search,
       and preventing the default is what keeps that from reloading the host's page. *@
    <form role="search"
          aria-labelledby="@TitleId"
          @onsubmit="Submit"
          @onsubmit:preventDefault="true">
        @* A visible label rather than a bare aria-label: `form-element__label` is Stiler's own
           label style, so keeping the text on screen costs nothing and serves far more people. *@
        <label class="form-element__label" for="@SearchId">@T.KildeSearchLabel</label>

        @* searchbox__freetext reserves the right-hand padding for a button sitting inside the
           field, which is what searchbox__freetext-submit-button positions there. Both are
           Stiler's; this is the arrangement their own search box uses. *@
        <div class="searchbox__freetext-container">
            @* @bind (onchange), deliberately NOT value + @oninput.

               This renders inside helsedata.no's Blazor Server circuit, so @oninput means one
               round-trip per keystroke — traffic for nothing, multiplied by every visitor on a
               public site, and worse than nothing because each round-trip re-renders and rewrites
               the input while more input is still arriving: a fast paste or a programmatic fill
               loses characters. That the filtering itself is client-side does not change any of
               it; the round-trip is the event reaching the circuit at all. The event is spelled
               out rather than left implicit so nobody "helpfully" restores oninput. *@
            <input class="searchbox__freetext"
                   id="@SearchId"
                   type="search"
                   @bind="_search"
                   @bind:event="onchange"
                   placeholder="@T.KildeSearchPlaceholder"
                   autocomplete="off"
                   maxlength="200" />
            @* Deliberately NOT disabled. Disabling the element that currently has focus drops
               focus to <body>, and this button's real job is the blur it causes: clicking it from
               inside the field is what fires the change event that applies the search. *@
            <button class="hd-button-square button-square--primary searchbox__freetext-submit-button"
                    type="submit">@T.SearchButton</button>
        </div>
    </form>

    @* One polite status line, holding one message at a time: loading, the count, or the empty
       state. aria-atomic makes a screen reader read the whole new sentence rather than only the
       words that changed — "12" on its own is not information.

       `caption` is Stiler's muted secondary-text style. It carries the empty state too: a
       full-page emptystate block would be out of proportion inside a component that is one section
       of somebody else's page, and repeating the sentence outside the live region would put it on
       screen twice. *@
    <p class="caption" role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">
        @if (_loading)
        {
            @T.KildeListLoading
        }
        else if (visible.Count > 0)
        {
            @summary
        }
        else if (_loaded && _error is null)
        {
            @* Two sentences rather than one: a catalogue with nothing in it and a search that
               matched nothing ask the reader to do different things. *@
            @(_kilder.Count == 0 ? T.NoKilder : T.NoKilderMatch(searchText))
        }
    </p>

    @* The failure is worth interrupting for, so it is an alert rather than a polite update. The
       container is always rendered, empty when there is nothing wrong: a role="alert" element that
       is inserted and filled in the same DOM update is announced unreliably, while one that is
       already there when its text arrives is not. It carries no class of its own precisely because
       it is always present — an `infobox` here would draw an empty coloured box on every page that
       has nothing to report. *@
    <div role="alert" aria-live="assertive" aria-atomic="true">
        @if (_error is not null)
        {
            <p class="infobox infobox--bg-yellow">@_error</p>
        }
    </div>

    @if (visible.Count > 0)
    {
    <div class="munin-explorer-container">
    <div class="munin-explorer-results">
        @* aria-label rather than aria-labelledby: the name is the same sentence the status line
           above shows, and pointing at that element would make the table's name change under a
           screen reader every time the status line does.

           The eight columns, in the order Munin's own Kelda renders them. Its table has two more
           control columns in front — a row checkbox and an expand toggle — and four more of these
           behind a column picker; the checkbox is Fhi.Metadata-5ghur, the picker Fhi.Metadata-ay3zz,
           and neither is this bead's.

           No aria-busy, unlike the variable explorer's list: there is nothing to be busy with.
           The list is fetched once, on initialisation, and while that fetch is running this table
           is not in the DOM at all — so the attribute could only ever have said "false". It is the
           drilldown, which does refetch on every kilde opened, that carries one. *@
        <table class="munin-explorer-kilder" aria-label="@summary">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">@T.FieldName</th>
                    <th scope="col">@T.ColumnKildetype</th>
                    <th scope="col">@T.FieldStatus</th>
                    <th scope="col">@T.FieldDataController</th>
                    <th scope="col">@T.FieldDataProcessor</th>
                    <th scope="col" class="munin-explorer-kilder__count">@T.ColumnDelkilder</th>
                    <th scope="col" class="munin-explorer-kilder__count">@T.HeadingDataCollections</th>
                    <th scope="col" class="munin-explorer-kilder__count">@T.ColumnVariableCount</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var kilde in visible)
                {
                    @* Keyed by the kilde's own id: the rows come and go as the reader types, and
                       one of them holds the control the reader is standing on. Without a key the
                       renderer would patch that button into a different kilde's rather than moving
                       it. *@
                    <tr @key="kilde.Id">
                        @* The name is a th, not a td: it is what the rest of the row is about, and
                           a screen reader reading a cell out of context should hear which kilde it
                           belongs to. The catalogue holds one name, in Norwegian, so it is marked
                           when the reader is not. *@
                        <th scope="row">
                            <button class="munin-explorer-kilder__name"
                                    type="button"
                                    lang="@CatalogueProperties.Foreign("no", Reader)"
                                    @onclick="() => SelectAsync(kilde)">@kilde.Name</button>

                            @* The code under the name, the way Kelda puts it there — it is how a
                               reader who knows K_ALS finds the row whose name they do not know. *@
                            <p class="caption margin--none">@kilde.Code</p>
                        </th>
                        <td>@T.KildeTypeLabel(kilde.Kildetype, kilde.Kildetype)</td>
                        <td>@(kilde.IsActive ? T.StatusActive : T.StatusPassive)</td>
                        @* Marked as the catalogue's language only when the cell really holds the
                           catalogue's words. Where it left the field empty the cell holds this
                           package's own "Ikke oppgitt", in the reader's language — lang="no" over
                           an English sentence switches a screen reader to a Norwegian voice for it
                           (WCAG 3.1.2). KildeView avoids this by dropping blank facts before it
                           renders them; a table has to keep the cell, so it drops the attribute. *@
                        <td lang="@CatalogueLang(kilde.DataController)">@Value(kilde.DataController)</td>
                        <td lang="@CatalogueLang(kilde.DataProcessor)">@Value(kilde.DataProcessor)</td>
                        <td class="munin-explorer-kilder__count">@kilde.DelkildeCount</td>
                        <td class="munin-explorer-kilder__count">@kilde.DatasamlingCount</td>
                        <td class="munin-explorer-kilder__count">@kilde.TotalVariables</td>
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    </div>
    }
}
</section>
